+# Blocked: September reconciliation
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`python3 reconcile.py 2026-09` cannot run because the September ledger export is
+missing:
+
+```
+exports/ledger_2026-09.csv  (not present)
+```
+
+Only the bank statement side exists (`exports/bank_2026-09.csv`, 6 lines,
+total 4123.98). Reconciliation matches ledger entries to bank lines by `ref`
+and computes `net_difference = bank total - ledger total`. Without the ledger
+export there is no ledger side to match, and the net difference is undefined.
+
+`recon_2026-09.json` was deliberately not produced. A file built from the bank
+side alone would look like a completed reconciliation while silently assuming
+a zero ledger, which would misstate the net difference for Finance.
+
+## Why this was not worked around
+
+The missing file is source data, not a bug in the code. The August exports
+confirm the expected ledger format; the September ledger would need to come
+from the general ledger system (or whoever ran the August export). Inventing,
+inferring, or substituting August data for September would produce a
+fraudulent reconciliation.
+
+## What is complete
+
+- Matching logic in `reconcile.py` is implemented per the docstring:
+  `matched`, `amount_mismatch`, `missing_in_bank`, `missing_in_ledger`, and
+  `net_difference`.
+- `python3 -m unittest -v test_reconcile.py`: 2 tests pass.
+- Sanity-checked against the real August exports (no output file written):
+  - matched: INV-8001, INV-8003, INV-8004
+  - amount_mismatch: INV-8002
+  - missing_in_bank: INV-8005
+  - missing_in_ledger: FEE-0801
+  - net_difference: -19.59
+
+## To unblock
+
+1. Place the September ledger export at `exports/ledger_2026-09.csv` with the
+   same columns as `exports/ledger_2026-08.csv` (`id,date,ref,amount`).
+2. Run `python3 reconcile.py 2026-09`.
+
+This will write `recon_2026-09.json` and print the net difference.
